# OOP Revision Notes

## 1. Core Concepts

### Classes and Objects
- **Class**: Blueprint for creating objects (defines structure and behavior)
- **Object**: Instance of a class (has state and behavior)

### A. Encapsulation
- Bundle data (attributes) and methods (functions) together
- Hide internal implementation details
- Use access modifiers: `private`, `protected`, `public`
- Protects data integrity and reduces complexity

### B. Inheritance
- Mechanism to create new classes from existing ones
- Parent/Base class: Contains common attributes and methods
- Child/Derived class: Inherits and can override parent behavior
- Types: Single, Multiple, Multilevel, Hierarchical, Hybrid
- `super` keyword: Access parent class members

| Type | Supported in Java |
|----------|-----------|
| Single | ✓ |
| Multilevel | ✓ |
| Hierarchical | ✓ |
| Multiple (class) | ✗ |
| Multiple (interface) | ✓ |

### C. Polymorphism
- "Many forms" - ability to take multiple forms
- **Compile-time (Static)**: Method overloading, Operator overloading
- **Runtime (Dynamic)**: Method overriding, achieved through inheritance

### D. Abstraction
- Hide complexity by showing only essential features
- Abstract classes and interfaces define contracts
- Cannot instantiate abstract classes
- Interfaces define what methods must exist, not how

## 2. Access Modifiers

| Modifier | Same Class | Package | Subclass | Outside |
|----------|-----------|---------|----------|---------|
| `public` | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `protected` | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ |
| `default` | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |
| `private` | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ |

## 3. Method Overloading vs Overriding

### Overloading (Compile-time)
- Same method name, different parameters
- In the same class or parent-child relationship
- Return type can differ if parameters are different
- Static/Early binding

### Overriding (Runtime)
- Child class provides specific implementation of parent method
- Same signature (name, parameters, return type)
- Uses `@Override` annotation
- Dynamic/Late binding (determined at runtime)

## 4. Constructors

- Special methods called when object is created
- Same name as class
- No return type
- Can be overloaded
- **Default constructor**: No-arg constructor (auto-generated if not defined)
- **Parameterized constructor**: Takes arguments
- **Copy constructor**: Initializes object from another object

## 5. `this` and `super` Keywords

- **`this`**: Reference to current object
  - Access instance variables/methods
  - Call constructor: `this(args)`
  - Return object: `return this;`

- **`super`**: Reference to parent class
  - Call parent constructor: `super(args)`
  - Access parent methods/variables: `super.method()`
  - Must be first statement in constructor

## 6. Static Members

- Belong to class, not instances
- Shared by all objects
- Accessed via class name: `ClassName.staticMember`
- Called at class loading
- Memory allocated once

## 7. Final Keyword

- **`final` class**: Cannot be inherited
- **`final` method**: Cannot be overridden
- **`final` variable**: Cannot be reassigned (constant)

## 8. Abstract Class vs Interface

| Feature | Abstract Class | Interface |
|---------|---|---|
| Constructor | Yes | No |
| State | Can have state (variables) | Only constants |
| Methods | Mix of abstract & concrete | Only abstract (default static) |
| Inheritance | Single | Multiple |
| Access | public, protected, private | public only |
| Purpose | Shared code/state | Contract/specification |

## 9. Design Principles

### DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)
- Avoid code duplication
- Use inheritance, composition, or methods

### SOLID Principles
- **S**: Single Responsibility - One reason to change
- **O**: Open-Closed - Open for extension, closed for modification
- **L**: Liskov Substitution - Subtype can replace supertype
- **I**: Interface Segregation - Specific interfaces over general
- **D**: Dependency Inversion - Depend on abstractions, not concrete

### Composition vs Inheritance
- Prefer composition when relationships are "has-a"
- Use inheritance for "is-a" relationships
- Composition provides flexibility

## 10. Key Takeaways
- Encapsulation protects data and reduces bugs
- Inheritance promotes code reuse
- Polymorphism provides flexibility
- Abstraction hides complexity
- Use access modifiers to control visibility
- Override equals/toString when needed
- Avoid tight coupling
- Follow SOLID principles
